Although this is not port alpha specific, I'd like to ask this anyway here 
since I need very urgend help with this.

For file server purposes I had set up a alpha DEC 3000/300. It has 256 MB RAM, 
two PMAZC-AA (dual fast scsi) and a bunch of disks.
For the root file system I used a 4 GB Barracuda. To build the file server 
area I put 7 IBM DDYS-T18350 disks into two external shelfs (Storage 
expansion boxes). The 7 IBM disks where configured into a raid 5 array. To 
have a clean setup I hardwired the drives into the kernel. I.e.

tcds0 at tc? slot 4 ...
asc0 at tcds0 chip 0
tcds1 at tc? slot 0 ...
asc1 at tcds1 chip 0
asc2 at tcds1 chip 1
:
:

and so on. The drives are coded like the following.

scsibus0 at asc0
scsibus1 at asc1
scsibus2 at asc2
scsibus3 at asc3
scsibus4 at asc4

sd0 at scsibus0 target 0 lun 0
sd1 at scsibus0 target 1 lun 0
:
sd10 at scsibus1 target 0 lun 0
sd11 at scsibus1 target 1 lun 0
:
sd20 at scsibus2 target 0 lun 0
sd21 at scsibus2 target 1 lun 0
:
  
sd30 at scsibus3 target 0 lun 0
sd31 at scsibus3 target 1 lun 0
:
  
sd40 at scsibus4 target 0 lun 0
sd41 at scsibus4 target 1 lun 0

(up to 6 drives per scsibus are hardwired this way). Then I set up the raid 
according to raidctl(5). The config file looks

START array
1 6 1
START disks
/dev/sd10b
/dev/sd11b
/dev/sd12b
/dev/sd30b
/dev/sd31b
/dev/sd32b
