/dev/sd13b
START layout
32 1 1 5
START queue
fifo 100

The disk where labelled identically with two partitions. Partition a is 1 
MByte, the rest is partition b.

Then I constructed the raid. Everything worked like a charm. Disklabelling the 
array went ok, the system was taken into production.

After 14 days the power of the sd1? drives went bad. The raid array wasn't 
accessible. A reboot didn't immediately take care of the problem. After 
opening the storage expansion and checking the cabling the drives came back 
to live. But now I can not configure the raid. raidctl -c /etc/raid0.conf 
raid0 does not configure the raid due to different modification counts of the 
disks in the raid. raidctl(5) produces kernel messages stating that:
sd10 has a modification counter of 239
sd11 has a modification counter of 239
sd12 has a modification counter of 230
sd30 has a modification counter of 239
sd31 has a modification counter of 239
sd32 has a modification counter of 238

Thus, the raid will not configure! Since I'm sure there hasn't been any data 
written to the file systems I am hoping this problem can be solved without 
reconfiguring the raid. The very problem is, that there is no recent backup 
of the data (I thought that a failure of two disks at the same time wouldn't 
happen). The backup of the data to a tape was the next step, but after my 
holiday. The file server worked for 14 days without a problem. And I do not 
believe that the 3 disks are defect. After checking all cabling and 
reconnecting everything the disks can be accessed with dd with no problem. So 
I assume there should be a possibility to bring this raid back online again 
without any data loss.
If required the logs (/var/log/messages) are available since the system setup.

So please help me with this problem.
