> > Hello,
> >
> > As long as you make 100% sure that you have not mixed up the columns in
> > any= way,
> > you can reinitialize the raid with -C and -I without data loss. Then
> > mount readonly, backup your data, do a fsck and finally raidctl -P.
>
> I'd note that one of those disks has a mod counter that is quite a
> bit different than the rest -- in particular, it looks like the RAID
> set has been configured (and then unconfigured) about 4 times from
> the time that sd12 went "bad".  Unless you can guarantee that nothing
> on the filesystem was changed in any of those 4 configurations, I'd
> use a non-existant disk in place of sd12.  With a difference of just
> 1, the mod counter for sd32 indicates that it's very likely to be as
> close to in-sync with the rest of the disks as you are going to get
> at this point...  So "raidctl -C" will work, but I'd recommend on not
> using sd12 in there, and building a degraded RAID set first... (then
> mount readonly, backup data, and fsck.  After that, you can worry
> about adding sd12 back into the mix, and rebuilding)
>
> (you havn't said which NetBSD version you're using, but if you're
> using 2.0_Beta or -current, the word "absent" can be used in place of
> "/dev/sd12", and should work fine..)
